\begin{abstract}
This paper specifies a groundwater module for the hybrid sustainability-systems architecture. It distinguishes fast and slow aquifer formations, solute mass, functional salinity/intrusion states, observations, institutional review, implementation, and safety constraints. Storage is a constitutive function of hydraulic head rather than an unrelated duplicate state; pumping and managed recharge are sampled controls rather than state coordinates; and cross-formational exchange is bidirectional and head-gradient driven. The paper frames the two-pool representation as a falsifiable competing physical hypothesis, not a universal ontology. It defines a boundary-relative water and solute ledger, a state-safe set, a state-control ecological-flow relation, compatible-state uncertainty, and an identification/validation programme. No calibrated basin result is claimed until a named basin, data boundary, uncertainty model, and preregistration are supplied.
\end{abstract}

\section{Scope and status}
This is a domain-module specification, not yet a calibrated case study. Its purpose is to provide an admissible model template against which a basin-specific implementation can be assessed. All basin-level thresholds, priors, boundary flows, institutional rules, and observations must be preregistered before fitting.

\section{Declared boundary, states, and units}
The accounting boundary is a named hydrogeological management region and its specified formations, rivers, managed imports/exports, and receiving environments. All water balances use volume; solutes use declared mass moieties. Water mass and dissolved-solute mass are not added.

The continuous state is
\[
Z=(H_f,H_s,M_q,\sigma_{\rm sal},\chi),
\]
where $H_f,H_s$ are hydraulic heads in fast and slow formations; $M_q$ is contaminant mass; $\sigma_{\rm sal}$ is a defined salinity/intrusion functional state; and $\chi$ denotes additional declared functional hydrological states. Water storage is not an independent duplicate coordinate:
\[
A_i=\mathcal A_i(H_i),\qquad C_i(H_i)=\frac{d\mathcal A_i}{dH_i}>0,
\quad i\in\{f,s\}.
\]
If this constitutive reduction is inadequate, the implementation must use an explicit DAE or distributed groundwater model and re-establish all accounting and safety conditions.

\section{Physical material and solute dynamics}
At decision time $t_k$, the institution prescribes
\[
a_k\in\Gamma(B_k,h_k),
\]
and implemented controls satisfy
\[
U_k=(q_{p,f,k},q_{p,s,k},q_{r,k},q_{{\rm rel},k})
\in\mathcal E(B_k,h_k,a_k).
\]
Controls are held between reviews by a preregistered zero-order hold or specified dispatch rule.

Cross-formational leakage is signed:
\[
\ell_{fs}=\kappa_{fs}(H_f-H_s).
\]
Its directional components are donor-limited:
\[
\ell_{f\to s}=[\ell_{fs}]_+\psi_f(A_f),\qquad
\ell_{s\to f}=[-\ell_{fs}]_+\psi_s(A_s),
\]
with $\psi_i(0)=0$, and $\ell^{\rm net}_{fs}=\ell_{f\to s}-\ell_{s\to f}$. A basin implementation may replace the linear Darcy approximation with a nonlinear conductance law, but must allow direction reversal when hydrogeology permits it.

The head-based water balances are
\begin{align}
C_f(H_f)\dot H_f&=R_{\rm nat}+q_r-q_{p,f}-\ell^{\rm net}_{fs}-L_f+J_f,\\
C_s(H_s)\dot H_s&=\ell^{\rm net}_{fs}-q_{p,s}-L_s+J_s,
\end{align}
where $R_{\rm nat}$, $L_i$, and $J_i$ are typed boundary/routing terms. Their sources, signs, units, uncertainty, and donor limits must be declared. The terms $J_i$ may include river exchange or imports/exports; they cannot be residual sinks.

A solute balance has the generic form
\[
\dot M_q=I_q-O_q(X,U,\omega)+\mathcal R_q(X,U,\omega),
\]
where concentration is derived only after water volume is defined. If salinity is represented as mass, it joins the material ledger; if represented as an intrusion/interface or damage index, it remains a functional state with an explicit map.

\section{Safety and admissible action}
The state-safe set is
\[
\mathcal C_Z(\lambda)=\{Z:H_f\ge H_f^{\min}(\lambda),\ H_s\ge H_s^{\min}(\lambda),\ M_q\le M_q^{\max}(\lambda),\ \sigma_{\rm sal}\le\sigma_{\max}(\lambda),\ \chi\in\mathcal X_\chi(\lambda)\}.
\]
Each threshold must state whether it represents accessibility, subsidence risk, ecosystem impairment, drinking-water quality, legal compliance, recovery risk, or a normative allocation choice.

An ecological-flow/service requirement is a state-control relation, not silently a state condition:
\[
\mathcal A(Z,\omega;\lambda)=\{U:Q_{\rm eco}(Z,U,\omega)\ge Q_{\rm eco}^{\min}(\lambda)\}.
\]
The module must check that $\mathcal C_Z$ and $\mathcal A$ are nonempty and compatible with physical, energy, legal, budget, and implementation limits.

\section{Observation, compatible states, and institutions}
Measurements may include multi-depth heads, abstraction meters, recharge observations, water quality, land subsidence, river flow, remote sensing, and institutional records. They are represented by
\[
Y_k=\mathcal O(Z_{[t_{k-1},t_k]})+\varepsilon_k.
\]
The compatible set
\[
B_k\subseteq C([t_{k-1}-\tau,t_k],\mathcal Z)\times\Theta
\]
contains states and parameters consistent with observations, prior controls, declared errors, and structural discrepancy assumptions. A proxy is not a state observation until its measurement relation and validation error are supplied. GRACE/G3P may be corroborative at coarse scale but is not by itself a local aquifer-state observation.

Institutional state $h_k$ records review rule, permit authority, monitoring capacity, enforcement, emergency status, and relevant allocation constraints. This permits the study of physical, epistemic, authority, and implementation infeasibility separately.

\section{Competing physical and institutional hypotheses}
\paragraph{Physical hypotheses.} Compare at least: (H0) one-pool storage; (H1) fast/slow two-pool storage with bidirectional leakage; and (H2) a justified distributed or higher-dimensional model. The two-pool hypothesis is supported only if it improves held-out state/service/safety prediction or gives demonstrably better calibrated uncertainty.

\paragraph{Institutional hypotheses.} Compare: scarcity-amplifying extraction; protective restraint/restoration; and inertia/capture/state-dependent action. No generic delay parameter is interpreted as an institutional lag without dated evidence of observation, assessment, authorization, implementation, and enforcement.

\section{Identification, discrepancy, and validation}
The fast/slow split requires independent constraints: geological geometry, multi-depth heads, pumping tests, tracer/isotope/water-age evidence where available, recharge estimates, and prior ranges for $C_i$ and $\kappa_{fs}$. Leakage may not absorb unexplained residuals.

Structural discrepancy is explicit:
\[
\dot Z=F_{\rm gw}(Z,U,\omega,\vartheta)+\delta_t,
\qquad \delta_t\in\mathcal D_t.
\]
Process uncertainty, parameter uncertainty, observation error, structural discrepancy, and implementation uncertainty are reported separately.

A basin preregistration must declare: boundary, units, state/control distinction, safe thresholds, observation/proxy model, institutional events, uncertainty semantics, comparators, residual tolerance, held-out period, and falsification criteria. If held-out data contain no safety crossing, the study must not claim calibrated rare-event crossing probabilities; it may report stress tests and explicitly extrapolative risk statements.

\section{Diagnostics and recovery}
Gross throughput time, local net-rate horizon, model-based crossing time, and probabilistic failure horizon are distinct. A finite gross draw is not evidence that storage is declining when recharge/recycling balances it. Recovery requires return not only of head or storage but of the compatible-state/institutional state to a viable set; a physically improved basin may remain institutionally ungovernable if uncertainty or authority constraints persist.

\section{Conclusion}
This module supplies a falsifiable, typed groundwater test of the general framework. It does not claim that every aquifer has two stores, that monitoring is sufficient for safety, or that a particular review interval is optimal. Its purpose is to determine whether complete accounting, explicit information constraints, and hybrid authority/implementation improve safety assessment relative to simpler alternatives.

